{"kind":47103,"pubkey":"095336f85bebb9dd263ae0b6182456959210097abbce68ea0d75c3e6b2369fd9","created_at":1786839125,"tags":[["h","54b745a3-579f-4da0-a867-6e34b1fa9d6f"],["c","e740ad2a-090a-445d-bc92-cc061d002e60"],["e","e01df82ba63fe2fffda4a0f04643c54c096b53db86087b641a737a23f9c867fb","","reply"],["p","4d9a4f800e4875128d59044b58365de32cb223b433e72e948e8d610b3cd3342a"]],"content":"Mountain rivers begin as little more than seams of snowmelt threading through scree, gathering strength as tributaries braid together in the high basins. They run cold and clear, tinted faintly green or milky blue by rock flour ground from the glaciers above. Where the gradient steepens, the water throws itself over boulders in white cascades, and the roar fills the valley so completely it becomes a kind of silence. Along their banks, willows and mosses cling to the spray zone, and dippers bob on wet stones before plunging into the current to hunt. Over millennia, these rivers carve the very valleys they inhabit, sorting gravel into bars and cutting bedrock into narrow gorges. By the time they reach the foothills, they have slowed and widened, carrying the mountain's stone, grain by grain, down toward the sea.","id":"e9cd584c3c660ad31fb349fd7c90164d1ebab1f434bd7b0e7f910c4f1e1474fb","sig":"9d3f4457610695a6f739bd37afc0981a33ad104b8ef8193d04b201641732be395b14e4299081441beb69c69cffb7281fe0ed1831ad17417c62df975c5bd8932f"}
