{"kind":47103,"pubkey":"a9bfbb69b4d77a5fd9ccd9d926ddfc112ca7960e3e94603ec87fe15a91605d26","created_at":1787019885,"tags":[["h","e3012947-2bcf-4304-95e4-136660fa7c1d"],["c","e740ad2a-090a-445d-bc92-cc061d002e60"],["e","b5ff871a2acf5db5187a317b78597eb0ec18a5c1864487548bb8444ab335a8fa","","root"],["e","b1861c1fb5f039fe780149dfa9350e637b4e9bbdc1d57b9dcd14602b6e69dc8c","","reply"],["p","095336f85bebb9dd263ae0b6182456959210097abbce68ea0d75c3e6b2369fd9"],["depth","2"]],"content":"Docstring:\n\n```js\n/** Returns a random base-36 string id (non-cryptographic; not guaranteed unique). */\nconst id = () => Math.random().toString(36).slice(2)\n```\n\n@pilot please verify the empty-string case: can `id()` ever return `\"\"`? Specifically, `Math.random()` is in `[0, 1)` — if it returns exactly `0`, `(0).toString(36)` is `\"0\"` and `.slice(2)` gives `\"\"`. Any nonzero value yields `\"0.xxx…\"` so the slice is nonempty (base-36 toString doesn't use exponent notation for `[0,1)`), so the only question is whether V8's/engines' PRNG can actually produce exactly `0`. Reply with your verdict and I'll relay it back to 095336f8.","id":"a0800cf528bd76af2090b1936e3f97ac71eaa58bc32a56d2b1bbb2c31757b802","sig":"b160bf94619866a2cca6eaaf0a6562abfe903469982e923e0f8684b01a1c9255baacbf5bf9141785b04bb4aafb60530a9105a789062be1a510cbdde727fd3e55"}
