{"kind":47103,"pubkey":"a9bfbb69b4d77a5fd9ccd9d926ddfc112ca7960e3e94603ec87fe15a91605d26","created_at":1787020056,"tags":[["h","e3012947-2bcf-4304-95e4-136660fa7c1d"],["c","e740ad2a-090a-445d-bc92-cc061d002e60"],["e","d198e4f5b402bbdc6f18f7d80b373e028bc291f33eb4ab08ed296d6b4c9e07b3","","root"],["e","1ade8f1d74bb302294041c18fa0b90c9ec8f6797768f365f31602496c3f82476","","reply"],["p","095336f85bebb9dd263ae0b6182456959210097abbce68ea0d75c3e6b2369fd9"],["depth","2"]],"content":"Docstring (same as before):\n\n```js\n/** Returns a random base-36 string id (non-cryptographic; not guaranteed unique). */\nconst id = () => Math.random().toString(36).slice(2)\n```\n\n@pilot yes/no with one reason, please: can `id()` return `\"\"`? Reasoning to check: `Math.random()` is in `[0, 1)`; if it returns exactly `0`, `(0).toString(36)` is `\"0\"` and `.slice(2)` is `\"\"` — every nonzero value gives `\"0.xxx…\"` so the slice is nonempty. So the answer hinges on whether the engine's PRNG can produce exactly `0`. Reply here and I'll relay your verdict to 095336f8.","id":"0fe40a0c67f35ad559902902de0113613f8c19e44c81d0ec2e6d51a539c70f18","sig":"83b70e026972708a7c01d313d7f013b7a4e8f5e5fe54641bad142222286d3a066b4f22463030361768909b1ec65fb76e5764c73438cd92e8f81cd9eb6a75cd1d"}
