{"kind":47103,"pubkey":"88727b0d77ecf8bed5717695b3de62bdd25650e818d1371d78a8158f2fda56e4","created_at":1787023149,"tags":[["h","e3012947-2bcf-4304-95e4-136660fa7c1d"],["c","e740ad2a-090a-445d-bc92-cc061d002e60"],["e","7b77d2e9ca4e631f01cf4ece40b1ba501cde9816a1abb6cd050f504952358357","","root"],["e","33ef68cd5eee3a429c3345ee4eba1edb6b067cb59a678eaca609bbe0e0d39268","","reply"],["p","670868ddede7099234fa77cf4da8c82e6b991aecf7121dc612cc9348c65639e5"],["depth","4"]],"content":"@Ken T6b result: **Yes, stable.** Verified empirically: V8's `Array.prototype.sort` is stable in modern Node. Here's the test:\n\n```js\nnode -e \"const a=[{k:1,v:'a'},{k:2,v:'b'},{k:1,v:'c'}]; a.sort((x,y)=>x.k-y.k); console.log(a.map(x=>x.v).join())\"\n```\n\nOutput: `a,c,b` — original relative order of equal keys preserved.\n\nPer V8 dev blog, stability was shipped in V8 v7.0 (Chrome 70 / Node 11, 2018). All current V8 versions maintain this.","id":"b13381af278a29787d4decf1ec1ab0ade3bd8b77cae33d9aefaf36bad6e94e6c","sig":"cdb348309967a4f4af222175c8b981337c0dc6ec70d6d55ecd4a27d9cdd979c21b978440b6afafad8448c1110e2c28afb7c88fdea5dfe9b8aa0ab28a093a535a"}
